{"topics":[{"id":"topic.tenses","title":"Verb tenses","summary":"How English verbs show time: present, past and perfect forms."},{"id":"topic.future","title":"Talking about the future","summary":"Will, going to, present forms and the other ways to talk about what comes next."},{"id":"topic.modals","title":"Modal verbs","summary":"Can, must, should, might and friends: ability, obligation, advice and likelihood."},{"id":"topic.conditionals","title":"Conditionals and wishes","summary":"If-sentences, real and imagined, and how to talk about wishes and regrets."},{"id":"topic.passive","title":"The passive and causatives","summary":"Putting the action first: passive forms, reporting passives and have something done."},{"id":"topic.reported-speech","title":"Reported speech","summary":"Telling people what someone else said, asked or suggested."},{"id":"topic.relative-clauses","title":"Relative clauses","summary":"Adding information about a noun with who, which, that, whose and more."},{"id":"topic.articles-determiners","title":"Articles and determiners","summary":"A, an, the, no article, and words like some, any, each and every."},{"id":"topic.adjectives-adverbs","title":"Adjectives and adverbs","summary":"Describing things and actions, word order and intensifiers."},{"id":"topic.comparison","title":"Comparison","summary":"Comparatives, superlatives and other ways to compare."},{"id":"topic.verb-patterns","title":"Verb patterns","summary":"Which verbs take -ing, which take to, and how objects fit in."},{"id":"topic.linking","title":"Linking ideas","summary":"Conjunctions, linkers and discourse markers that connect ideas."},{"id":"topic.clauses","title":"Clauses and sentence structure","summary":"Time, purpose and participle clauses, and how sentences are built."},{"id":"topic.emphasis","title":"Emphasis and style","summary":"Inversion, cleft sentences, fronting and choosing a register."},{"id":"topic.vocabulary","title":"Vocabulary","summary":"Everyday and academic words with definitions and example sentences."},{"id":"topic.listening","title":"Listening","summary":"Dictation with real sentences, to train your ear."}],"grammarPoints":[{"id":"gp.articles-in-general-statements","level":"B2","topicId":"topic.articles-determiners","title":"Articles in general statements","summary":"Learn when to use 'the' or no article at all when talking about things in general, including institutions like school or hospital.","explanation":"When we talk about things in general, we often use articles differently than when we talk about specific items. For *plural countable nouns* and *uncountable nouns*, we usually use no article (the zero article) to make a general statement.\n\nHowever, for a *singular countable noun*, we can use 'the' to refer to an entire class or species. This is called the generic 'the'. It treats one example as representative of all of them.\n\nFinally, with certain institutions like *school*, *hospital*, *church*, *prison*, or *work*, we use the zero article when we are talking about their *purpose* or the activity that happens there. If we are talking about the specific building itself, we use 'the'.","patterns":["Zero article + plural countable noun (general)","Zero article + uncountable noun (general)","The + singular countable noun (generic class)","Zero article + institution (purpose)"],"examples":[{"text":"Dogs are loyal companions.","note":"No article is used because 'dogs' refers to all dogs in general.","source":{"kind":"generated"}},{"text":"Water is essential for life.","note":"No article is used because 'water' is an uncountable noun used in a general sense.","source":{"kind":"generated"}},{"text":"The internet has changed how we communicate.","note":"'The internet' refers to the concept or system as a whole, representing its class.","source":{"kind":"generated"}},{"text":"My cousin is still in school.","note":"No article is used because 'school' refers to the activity of studying, not a specific building.","source":{"kind":"generated"}},{"text":"The hospital across the street is very modern.","note":"'The hospital' refers to a specific building, not the general concept of receiving medical care.","source":{"kind":"generated"}},{"text":"Success in school calls for hard study.","source":{"kind":"tatoeba","sentenceId":21636,"author":"Zifre"}},{"text":"Do you like the piano?","source":{"kind":"tatoeba","sentenceId":69787,"author":"CK"}}],"commonErrors":[{"wrong":"The happiness is important.","right":"Happiness is important.","note":"Do not use 'the' with uncountable nouns when speaking generally."},{"wrong":"I love cat.","right":"I love the cat. / I love cats.","note":"Use 'the' for a generic singular noun or no article for a general plural noun."},{"wrong":"She went to the hospital for an emergency.","right":"She went to hospital for an emergency.","note":"Use the zero article when referring to the purpose of an institution."}],"related":["gp.each-every-all","gp.articles-with-abstract-nouns","gp.both-either-neither"]},{"id":"gp.be-used-to-and-get-used-to","level":"B2","topicId":"topic.verb-patterns","title":"Be used to and get used to","summary":"Learn to talk about being familiar with something or becoming familiar with it using 'be used to' and 'get used to'.","explanation":"We use *be used to* + noun or verb-ing to say that something is normal or familiar for us. It describes a state of being accustomed to something.\n\nWe use *get used to* + noun or verb-ing to talk about the process of becoming familiar with something. It describes the change from something being new or difficult to becoming normal or easy.\n\nBoth 'be used to' and 'get used to' are followed by a noun, a pronoun, or a verb in the -ing form (gerund). The 'to' here is a preposition, not part of an infinitive.","patterns":["Subject + be + used to + noun/pronoun","Subject + be + used to + verb-ing","Subject + get + used to + noun/pronoun","Subject + get + used to + verb-ing"],"examples":[{"text":"I'm used to the city noise now; it doesn't bother me.","note":"Describes a current state of familiarity with city noise.","source":{"kind":"generated"}},{"text":"It took her a while to get used to working from home.","note":"Describes the process of becoming familiar with a new work situation.","source":{"kind":"generated"}},{"text":"Are you used to eating spicy food?","note":"Asks about someone's current familiarity with spicy food.","source":{"kind":"generated"}},{"text":"My dog is getting used to his new bed.","note":"Describes an ongoing process of the dog becoming familiar with something new.","source":{"kind":"generated"}},{"text":"They weren't used to the cold weather in Canada.","note":"Describes a past state of not being familiar with the cold weather.","source":{"kind":"generated"}},{"text":"It'll take some time to get used to wearing a wig.","source":{"kind":"tatoeba","sentenceId":1096398,"author":"CK"}},{"text":"Tom didn't seem to be used to doing that.","source":{"kind":"tatoeba","sentenceId":6343824,"author":"CK"}}],"commonErrors":[{"wrong":"I am used to work long hours.","right":"I am used to working long hours.","note":"After 'be used to' or 'get used to', use the -ing form of the verb."},{"wrong":"She used to the quiet life.","right":"She is used to the quiet life.","note":"Remember to include 'be' or 'get' before 'used to' when talking about familiarity."},{"wrong":"It's hard to be used to waking up early.","right":"It's hard to get used to waking up early.","note":"Use 'get used to' for the process of becoming familiar, especially when it's difficult."}],"related":["gp.remember-stop-try-forget","gp.gerund-or-infinitive","gp.phrasal-verbs-basics"]},{"id":"gp.causative-have-and-get","level":"B2","topicId":"topic.passive","title":"Have and get something done","summary":"Learn to use 'have something done' and 'get something done' to talk about arranging for someone else to perform a service for you.","explanation":"When you *have something done* or *get something done*, it means you arrange for another person to do a task or service for you, rather than doing it yourself. This structure is very common when talking about professional services like repairs, cleaning, or beauty treatments.\n\nThe main difference between 'have' and 'get' in this structure is often one of formality. 'Have something done' is generally more neutral or formal, while 'get something done' can be slightly more informal or conversational. Both are grammatically correct and widely used.\n\nRemember that the verb after the object is always in the *past participle* form. The focus is on the result of the action, not who performed it.","patterns":["subject + have + object + past participle","subject + get + object + past participle"],"examples":[{"text":"I had my car serviced last week.","note":"This means someone else serviced the car for me.","source":{"kind":"generated"}},{"text":"She's getting her hair cut tomorrow.","note":"She is arranging for a hairdresser to cut her hair.","source":{"kind":"generated"}},{"text":"We need to have the broken window replaced.","note":"We need to arrange for someone to replace the window.","source":{"kind":"generated"}},{"text":"He got his teeth whitened at the dentist.","note":"The dentist whitened his teeth, not him.","source":{"kind":"generated"}},{"text":"They are having a new fence built in their backyard.","note":"They hired someone to build the fence.","source":{"kind":"generated"}}],"commonErrors":[{"wrong":"I had repaired my computer.","right":"I had my computer repaired.","note":"The object 'my computer' comes before the past participle 'repaired'."},{"wrong":"She got clean her apartment.","right":"She got her apartment cleaned.","note":"Use the past participle 'cleaned', not the base form 'clean'."},{"wrong":"We will have painted the house.","right":"We will have the house painted.","note":"The structure is 'have + object + past participle', not 'have + past participle + object'."}],"related":["gp.passive-all-tenses","gp.reporting-passive","gp.advanced-passive-forms"]},{"id":"gp.despite-and-in-spite-of","level":"B2","topicId":"topic.linking","title":"Despite, in spite of and whereas","summary":"Use \"despite,\" \"in spite of,\" and \"whereas\" to show contrast between ideas or facts, whether surprising or direct.","explanation":"Despite and in spite of are used to show a contrast that is surprising or unexpected. They are followed by a noun, a pronoun, or a verb in the -ing form. Both \"despite\" and \"in spite of\" have the same meaning and can be used interchangeably.\n\nWhereas is used to show a direct contrast or comparison between two facts or ideas. It connects two clauses, highlighting how they are different. It often means \"in contrast\" or \"while.\"","patterns":["Despite / In spite of + noun phrase","Despite / In spite of + -ing form","Whereas + clause"],"examples":[{"text":"Despite the heavy rain, the outdoor market was still busy.","note":"Shows unexpected contrast with a noun phrase.","source":{"kind":"generated"}},{"text":"In spite of feeling nervous, she delivered an excellent speech.","note":"Shows unexpected contrast with an -ing form.","source":{"kind":"generated"}},{"text":"My sister prefers sweet desserts, whereas I always choose savory snacks.","note":"Shows direct contrast between two facts.","source":{"kind":"generated"}},{"text":"They decided to go camping, despite the forecast for cold weather.","note":"Shows unexpected contrast with a noun phrase.","source":{"kind":"generated"}},{"text":"Some people enjoy city life, whereas others prefer the quiet of the countryside.","note":"Shows direct contrast between two facts.","source":{"kind":"generated"}},{"text":"She went to school in spite of the pain in her leg.","source":{"kind":"tatoeba","sentenceId":315605,"author":"CK"}},{"text":"Despite having lived near the sea, she still cannot swim.","source":{"kind":"tatoeba","sentenceId":22241,"author":"Objectivesea"}}],"commonErrors":[{"wrong":"Despite of the noise, I managed to concentrate.","right":"Despite the noise, I managed to concentrate.","note":"Do not use \"of\" after \"despite.\""},{"wrong":"In spite the bad connection, we finished the video call.","right":"In spite of the bad connection, we finished the video call.","note":"Always use \"of\" after \"in spite.\""},{"wrong":"He likes to read, despite his friend watches movies.","right":"He likes to read, whereas his friend watches movies.","note":"\"Despite\" is followed by a noun or -ing form, not a clause. Use \"whereas\" for contrasting clauses."}],"related":["gp.although-however-though","gp.concession-clauses","gp.discourse-markers"]},{"id":"gp.each-every-all","level":"B2","topicId":"topic.articles-determiners","title":"Each, every and all","summary":"Learn to use 'each', 'every', and 'all' to talk about all members of a group, focusing on individual items or the group as a whole.","explanation":"We use *each*, *every*, and *all* to refer to all members of a group. While they all convey a sense of completeness, they are used differently depending on whether we are focusing on individuals or the group as a whole, and on the type of noun.\n\n*Each* focuses on individual items within a group, one by one. It is always followed by a singular countable noun. For example, \"Each participant received a badge.\" You can also use \"each of the\" before a plural noun or pronoun: \"Each of the books was interesting.\"\n\n*Every* refers to all members of a group as a whole, emphasizing completeness. It is also always followed by a singular countable noun. For example, \"Every car needs fuel.\" Unlike 'each', 'every' cannot be directly followed by 'of the' + plural noun/pronoun (you would say \"every one of the\"). 'Every' usually implies a group of three or more.\n\n*All* refers to the entire group, emphasizing the totality. It can be followed by plural countable nouns or uncountable nouns. For example, \"All the students passed the exam\" or \"All the water evaporated.\" You can also use \"all of the\" before plural nouns, uncountable nouns, or pronouns: \"All of the ingredients are fresh.\"","patterns":["Each + singular countable noun","Every + singular countable noun","All + plural countable noun","All + uncountable noun","Each of the + plural noun/pronoun","All of the + plural noun/uncountable noun/pronoun"],"examples":[{"text":"Each flower in the garden has a unique color.","note":"Focuses on individual flowers, one by one.","source":{"kind":"generated"}},{"text":"Every employee attended the mandatory training session.","note":"Refers to all employees as a complete group.","source":{"kind":"generated"}},{"text":"All the windows were cleaned before the party.","note":"Refers to the entire group of windows.","source":{"kind":"generated"}},{"text":"All the equipment needs to be checked regularly.","note":"Refers to the entire amount of uncountable equipment.","source":{"kind":"generated"}},{"text":"Each of the proposals was carefully reviewed by the committee.","note":"Highlights individual proposals from a larger group.","source":{"kind":"generated"}},{"text":"In the house of his father, every prince is great.","source":{"kind":"tatoeba","sentenceId":3893153,"author":"AlanF_US"}},{"text":"All the seats are reserved.","source":{"kind":"tatoeba","sentenceId":5596083,"author":"zumley"}}],"commonErrors":[{"wrong":"Every of the students passed the test.","right":"Each of the students passed the test.","note":"Use 'each of the' or 'every one of the' before a plural noun."},{"wrong":"Each cars need regular maintenance.","right":"Each car needs regular maintenance.","note":"'Each' is followed by a singular countable noun."},{"wrong":"All the student received a certificate.","right":"All the students received a certificate.","note":"'All' with countable nouns is followed by a plural noun."}],"related":["gp.articles-in-general-statements","gp.articles-with-abstract-nouns","gp.both-either-neither"]},{"id":"gp.emphatic-do","level":"B2","topicId":"topic.emphasis","title":"Emphatic do","summary":"Use 'do', 'does', or 'did' before a main verb to add emphasis, confirming that something is true, often in response to doubt or a contradiction.","explanation":"We use *emphatic do* (do, does, or did) to stress that a statement is true, especially when someone might doubt it or has said the opposite. It adds strength to your words, making your affirmation more forceful.\n\nThis structure is common when you want to correct a misunderstanding, express strong feelings, or confirm a fact. The auxiliary verb (do, does, or did) comes before the main verb, which is always in its base form.\n\nFor example, if someone says, \"You don't like vegetables,\" you might reply, \"I *do* like vegetables!\" to strongly contradict them. It's a way to show conviction in what you're saying.","patterns":["Subject + do + base verb (for I, you, we, they)","Subject + does + base verb (for he, she, it)","Subject + did + base verb (for all subjects in the past)"],"examples":[{"text":"I do enjoy cooking for my friends.","note":"Emphasizes the speaker's enjoyment, perhaps correcting a misconception.","source":{"kind":"generated"}},{"text":"She does understand the new software.","note":"Confirms her understanding, possibly against someone's doubt.","source":{"kind":"generated"}},{"text":"They did finish the report before the deadline.","note":"Stresses that the action was completed, contradicting a belief it wasn't.","source":{"kind":"generated"}},{"text":"It does seem like a good idea to me.","note":"Highlights the speaker's opinion, adding conviction.","source":{"kind":"generated"}},{"text":"We did try our best to solve the problem.","note":"Emphasizes the effort made, perhaps in a difficult situation.","source":{"kind":"generated"}}],"commonErrors":[{"wrong":"He does likes to read sci-fi novels.","right":"He does like to read sci-fi novels.","note":"After 'do', 'does', or 'did', the main verb must be in its base form."},{"wrong":"I do am tired after that long flight.","right":"I am tired after that long flight.","note":"Emphatic 'do' is not used with the verb 'to be'."},{"wrong":"They did went to the concert last night.","right":"They did go to the concert last night.","note":"Use 'did' for past tense emphasis, but the main verb remains in its base form."}],"related":["gp.it-cleft-sentences","gp.fronting-for-emphasis","gp.inversion-after-negative-adverbials"]},{"id":"gp.future-continuous","level":"B2","topicId":"topic.future","title":"Future continuous","summary":"Use the future continuous to talk about actions that will be in progress at a specific time or over a period in the future.","explanation":"The future continuous describes an action that will be ongoing at a particular moment or over a period in the future. It emphasizes the duration of the action rather than its completion.\n\nWe often use it to set the scene for another future event, or to talk about something that will be happening when something else occurs. It can also be used to ask politely about someone's future plans.\n\nThink of it as looking back from a point in the future and seeing an action in progress. For example, \"This time tomorrow, I'll be flying to Rome\" means that at that exact moment, the action of flying will be happening.","patterns":["Subject + will be + verb-ing","Subject + will not be + verb-ing (won't be)","Will + subject + be + verb-ing?"],"examples":[{"text":"This time next week, I'll be relaxing on the beach.","note":"Describes an action that will be in progress at a specific future time.","source":{"kind":"generated"}},{"text":"They won't be attending the meeting tomorrow morning.","note":"Shows an action that will not be ongoing in the future.","source":{"kind":"generated"}},{"text":"Will you be working late tonight?","note":"Asks about an ongoing activity or plan in the future.","source":{"kind":"generated"}},{"text":"When you call, I'll probably be cooking dinner.","note":"Indicates an action that will be in progress when another future event happens.","source":{"kind":"generated"}},{"text":"By 5 PM, she will be finishing her presentation.","note":"Refers to an action that will be ongoing up to or at a specific future point.","source":{"kind":"generated"}},{"text":"He will be playing tennis tomorrow afternoon.","source":{"kind":"tatoeba","sentenceId":304085,"author":"CK"}},{"text":"Someone will be meeting you.","source":{"kind":"tatoeba","sentenceId":5088449,"author":"CK"}}],"commonErrors":[{"wrong":"Tomorrow at 3 PM, I will work.","right":"Tomorrow at 3 PM, I will be working.","note":"Use future continuous for an action ongoing at a specific future time."},{"wrong":"When you arrive, I will cook.","right":"When you arrive, I will be cooking.","note":"Use future continuous for an action in progress when another event happens."},{"wrong":"She won't be finish her report by Friday.","right":"She won't be finishing her report by Friday.","note":"Remember to use the -ing form after 'be'."}],"related":["gp.future-perfect","gp.future-in-the-past","gp.going-to-future"]},{"id":"gp.future-perfect","level":"B2","topicId":"topic.future","title":"Future perfect","summary":"Use the future perfect to talk about actions that will be completed before a specific time or event in the future.","explanation":"The future perfect tense describes an action that will be finished *before* a certain point in the future. It emphasizes the completion of an action by a future deadline or event.\n\nWe often use time expressions like *by*, *by the time*, *before*, or *in X years/months* with the future perfect to indicate the future completion point.\n\nThink of it as looking back from a future moment and seeing that an action has already happened. It's not about when the action starts, but when it will be done.","patterns":["Subject + will have + past participle","Subject + will not have + past participle (won't have)","Will + subject + have + past participle?"],"examples":[{"text":"By 2025, they will have built the new bridge.","note":"The building will be completed before the year 2025.","source":{"kind":"generated"}},{"text":"I won't have finished my report by the time you get home.","note":"The report will still be incomplete when you arrive.","source":{"kind":"generated"}},{"text":"Will you have eaten dinner before the movie starts?","note":"Asking if the action of eating will be completed before a future event.","source":{"kind":"generated"}},{"text":"She will have lived in Paris for five years next month.","note":"The action of living will reach a five-year duration by next month.","source":{"kind":"generated"}},{"text":"The team will have practiced for hours by the end of the day.","note":"The practice will be completed, lasting for hours, by the day's end.","source":{"kind":"generated"}},{"text":"I will have finished the work by next week.","source":{"kind":"tatoeba","sentenceId":262143,"author":"CK"}},{"text":"You will have guessed its meaning by the end of the chapter.","source":{"kind":"tatoeba","sentenceId":69957,"author":"CM"}}],"commonErrors":[{"wrong":"By next week, I will finish the project.","right":"By next week, I *will have finished* the project.","note":"Use future perfect to show completion by a future time."},{"wrong":"They will have went to the store already.","right":"They *will have gone* to the store already.","note":"Always use the past participle form of the main verb."},{"wrong":"She has have completed her work by then.","right":"She *will have completed* her work by then.","note":"The auxiliary verb for future perfect is 'will have', not 'has have'."}],"related":["gp.future-continuous","gp.future-in-the-past","gp.going-to-future"]},{"id":"gp.gradable-and-extreme-adjectives","level":"B2","topicId":"topic.adjectives-adverbs","title":"Very or absolutely","summary":"Learn to use 'very' with gradable adjectives and 'absolutely' with extreme adjectives to intensify their meaning.","explanation":"We use intensifiers like 'very' and 'absolutely' to make adjectives stronger. The choice depends on the type of adjective.\n\n*Gradable adjectives* describe qualities that can vary in degree (e.g., good, tired, big, cold). With these, we use *'very'* to make them stronger. For example, 'very good' or 'very tired'.\n\n*Extreme adjectives* describe qualities that are already at their maximum degree (e.g., fantastic, exhausted, enormous, freezing). These adjectives already mean 'very + gradable adjective'. For example, 'fantastic' means 'very good', and 'exhausted' means 'very tired'. With extreme adjectives, we use *'absolutely'* (or 'completely', 'totally') to emphasize them. For example, 'absolutely fantastic' or 'absolutely exhausted'. You cannot use 'very' with extreme adjectives.","patterns":["very + gradable adjective","absolutely + extreme adjective","absolutely + gradable adjective (for strong emphasis)"],"examples":[{"text":"The movie was very interesting, so I watched it twice.","note":"'Interesting' is a gradable adjective, intensified by 'very'.","source":{"kind":"generated"}},{"text":"The view from the mountain top was absolutely breathtaking.","note":"'Breathtaking' is an extreme adjective, intensified by 'absolutely'.","source":{"kind":"generated"}},{"text":"I'm very hungry; I haven't eaten all day.","note":"'Hungry' is a gradable adjective, intensified by 'very'.","source":{"kind":"generated"}},{"text":"After the long hike, I was absolutely starving.","note":"'Starving' is an extreme adjective (meaning 'very hungry'), intensified by 'absolutely'.","source":{"kind":"generated"}},{"text":"That's absolutely true! I agree with you completely.","note":"'True' is a gradable adjective, but 'absolutely' is used here for strong emphasis.","source":{"kind":"generated"}},{"text":"Time has passed very fast.","source":{"kind":"tatoeba","sentenceId":2217,"author":"Zifre"}},{"text":"It's absolutely impossible for me to go on like this.","source":{"kind":"tatoeba","sentenceId":54702,"author":"CM"}}],"commonErrors":[{"wrong":"The weather was very freezing yesterday.","right":"The weather was absolutely freezing yesterday.","note":"'Freezing' is an extreme adjective, so it needs 'absolutely', not 'very'."},{"wrong":"I'm absolutely tired after running a marathon.","right":"I'm absolutely exhausted after running a marathon.","note":"'Tired' is gradable; 'exhausted' is extreme. Use 'absolutely' with 'exhausted'."},{"wrong":"Her new car is absolutely nice.","right":"Her new car is very nice.","note":"'Nice' is a gradable adjective, so 'very' is the correct intensifier."}],"related":["gp.order-of-adjectives","gp.so-and-such","gp.adverbs-of-frequency"]},{"id":"gp.had-better","level":"B2","topicId":"topic.modals","title":"Had better","summary":"Use 'had better' to give strong advice or warnings, often implying a negative consequence if the advice is not followed.","explanation":"We use *had better* to give strong advice or a warning. It suggests that there will be a negative consequence if the advice is not followed. It's stronger than 'should' and often refers to an immediate or specific situation.\n\nEven though it uses 'had', it refers to the present or future, not the past. The 'had' is almost always contracted to 'd, especially in spoken English (e.g., 'You'd better go').\n\nTo form the negative, we put 'not' after 'had better': *had better not*. We use the base form of the verb after 'had better' or 'had better not'.","patterns":["Subject + had better + base verb","Subject + 'd better + base verb","Subject + had better not + base verb","Subject + 'd better not + base verb"],"examples":[{"text":"You'd better finish that report by five o'clock.","note":"This is a strong warning about a deadline.","source":{"kind":"generated"}},{"text":"We had better leave now, or we'll miss the train.","note":"Giving urgent advice to avoid a negative outcome.","source":{"kind":"generated"}},{"text":"She'd better not forget her passport for the trip.","note":"A warning about a potential problem.","source":{"kind":"generated"}},{"text":"They had better apologize for their behavior.","note":"Strong advice about what someone should do.","source":{"kind":"generated"}},{"text":"I'd better call my parents; they might be worried.","note":"Expressing a strong personal obligation or necessity.","source":{"kind":"generated"}},{"text":"You'd better go home as soon as possible.","source":{"kind":"tatoeba","sentenceId":16505,"author":"CK"}},{"text":"You'd better hurry. If you don't, you'll be late.","source":{"kind":"tatoeba","sentenceId":4663007,"author":"CK"}}],"commonErrors":[{"wrong":"You have better study for the exam.","right":"You had better study for the exam.","note":"Always use 'had' (or 'd) with 'better', not 'have'."},{"wrong":"We had better to hurry.","right":"We had better hurry.","note":"Use the base form of the verb directly after 'had better', without 'to'."},{"wrong":"He had not better be late.","right":"He had better not be late.","note":"For the negative, 'not' comes after 'had better'."}],"related":["gp.modal-perfects-for-deduction","gp.should-have-and-could-have","gp.dont-have-to-vs-mustnt"]},{"id":"gp.it-cleft-sentences","level":"B2","topicId":"topic.emphasis","title":"It-cleft sentences","summary":"It-cleft sentences emphasize a specific part of a sentence, like a person, thing, time, or reason, using the structure 'It + be + emphasized part + relative clause'.","explanation":"We use *it-cleft sentences* to draw attention to a particular piece of information in a sentence. This structure makes that specific detail stand out, making it the main focus of the message. It's a way to add emphasis, often in spoken English or when you want to correct a misunderstanding.\n\nThe basic structure starts with 'It' followed by a form of 'be' (is/was). After 'be', you place the part of the sentence you want to emphasize. This emphasized part is then followed by a relative pronoun like 'who', 'that', or 'which', which introduces the rest of the sentence as a clause.\n\nFor example, if you say \"Sam fixed the computer,\" it's a simple statement. But if you say, \"*It was Sam who fixed the computer*,\" you are specifically highlighting Sam as the person responsible, perhaps because someone thought it was someone else, or because Sam's effort was particularly noteworthy.","patterns":["It + is/was + [person] + who/that + [verb phrase]","It + is/was + [thing] + that/which + [verb phrase]","It + is/was + [time/place/reason] + that + [clause]"],"examples":[{"text":"It was *the new software* that caused the system crash.","note":"Emphasizes the specific thing that caused the problem.","source":{"kind":"generated"}},{"text":"It's *my sister* who always remembers everyone's birthday.","note":"Highlights the person who performs the action.","source":{"kind":"generated"}},{"text":"It was *last Tuesday* that we finally signed the contract.","note":"Focuses on the specific time an event happened.","source":{"kind":"generated"}},{"text":"It's *in the main office* that you'll find the meeting schedule.","note":"Draws attention to the location of something.","source":{"kind":"generated"}},{"text":"It was *because of the heavy traffic* that I arrived late.","note":"Emphasizes the reason for an action or event.","source":{"kind":"generated"}},{"text":"It was Alice who went to the concert yesterday.","source":{"kind":"tatoeba","sentenceId":244386,"author":"sundown"}},{"text":"I forgot it was Tom who taught you how to play the guitar.","source":{"kind":"tatoeba","sentenceId":3126716,"author":"CK"}}],"commonErrors":[{"wrong":"It was my friend *which* helped me.","right":"It was my friend *who* helped me.","note":"Use 'who' or 'that' for people, not 'which'."},{"wrong":"It *are* the small details that make a difference.","right":"It *is* the small details that make a difference.","note":"The verb 'be' always agrees with 'it' (singular), not the emphasized plural noun."},{"wrong":"Was the manager who approved the plan.","right":"*It was* the manager who approved the plan.","note":"An 'it-cleft' sentence must begin with 'It'."}],"related":["gp.emphatic-do","gp.fronting-for-emphasis","gp.inversion-after-negative-adverbials"]},{"id":"gp.its-time-and-would-rather","level":"B2","topicId":"topic.conditionals","title":"It's time, would rather","summary":"Use 'it's time' and 'would rather' with past tense forms to express that something should happen now, or to state a preference for the present or future.","explanation":"We use the structure *It's time + subject + past simple* to say that something should happen now, or that it's already a bit late for it to happen. Even though we use a past simple verb, the meaning refers to the present or near future.\n\nWe use *would rather + subject + past simple* to express a preference for someone else to do (or not do) something in the present or future. Again, the past simple verb refers to present or future time, not the past.\n\nIf you are talking about your own preference, you can use *would rather + base verb* (without 'to'). For example, \"I'd rather stay home.\" This structure doesn't use a past form.","patterns":["It's time + subject + past simple","would rather + subject + past simple","would rather + base verb"],"examples":[{"text":"It's time we left for the concert.","note":"Expresses that we should leave now; 'left' refers to the present/future.","source":{"kind":"generated"}},{"text":"I'd rather you didn't tell anyone about our plans.","note":"Shows a preference for someone else's action in the present/future.","source":{"kind":"generated"}},{"text":"It's time the children went to bed.","note":"Indicates that the children should go to bed now.","source":{"kind":"generated"}},{"text":"Would you rather I ordered takeout tonight?","note":"Asks about a preference for someone else's action in the present/future.","source":{"kind":"generated"}},{"text":"I'd rather walk than take the bus.","note":"Expresses a personal preference using the base verb.","source":{"kind":"generated"}}],"commonErrors":[{"wrong":"It's time we go home.","right":"It's time we *went* home.","note":"Use the past simple after 'It's time + subject'."},{"wrong":"She'd rather he doesn't call her late.","right":"She'd rather he *didn't call* her late.","note":"Use the past simple (negative 'didn't + base verb') after 'would rather + subject'."},{"wrong":"I'd rather to eat pasta.","right":"I'd rather *eat* pasta.","note":"Use the base form of the verb directly after 'would rather' for personal preferences."}],"related":["gp.mixed-conditionals","gp.third-conditional","gp.unless-and-as-long-as"]},{"id":"gp.mixed-conditionals","level":"B2","topicId":"topic.conditionals","title":"Mixed conditionals","summary":"Mixed conditionals combine past and present tenses in 'if' sentences to show how a past action affects the present, or how a present situation affects the past.","explanation":"Mixed conditionals allow us to talk about hypothetical situations where the condition and the result happen at different times. We often use them to connect a past event or decision to a present consequence, or a present general truth to a past outcome.\n\nOne common type describes a *past condition* and its *present result*. For example, if something had happened differently in the past, the situation now would be different. The 'if' clause uses the past perfect, and the main clause uses 'would/could/might' + base verb.\n\nAnother type describes a *present or general condition* and its *past result*. This means if a current situation or characteristic were different, a past event would have turned out differently. The 'if' clause uses the simple past (often 'were' for all subjects), and the main clause uses 'would/could/might' + 'have' + past participle.","patterns":["If + past perfect, would/could/might + base verb","If + simple past (or 'were'), would/could/might + have + past participle"],"examples":[{"text":"If I had taken that job offer, I would be living in a different city now.","note":"A past decision (not taking the job) has a present consequence (living here).","source":{"kind":"generated"}},{"text":"If she weren't so shy, she would have spoken up at the meeting yesterday.","note":"A present characteristic (being shy) affected a past action (not speaking up).","source":{"kind":"generated"}},{"text":"If he had remembered his umbrella, he wouldn't be wet right now.","note":"A past action (forgetting the umbrella) has a present result (being wet).","source":{"kind":"generated"}},{"text":"If we didn't have this deadline, we would have spent more time on the design.","note":"A present situation (having a deadline) influenced a past action (spending less time).","source":{"kind":"generated"}},{"text":"If they had trained harder, they might be champions today.","note":"A past effort (training) could have led to a present status (being champions).","source":{"kind":"generated"}},{"text":"If I had been in time for the train, I would be there now.","source":{"kind":"tatoeba","sentenceId":31070,"author":"CK"}},{"text":"If he had trained himself harder at that time, he would be healthier now.","source":{"kind":"tatoeba","sentenceId":68696,"author":"CM"}}],"commonErrors":[{"wrong":"If I would have known, I would be there.","right":"If I had known, I would be there.","note":"Do not use 'would have' in the 'if' clause."},{"wrong":"If she was taller, she would have reached the shelf now.","right":"If she were taller, she would have reached the shelf now.","note":"Use 'were' for all subjects in the 'if' clause when talking about hypothetical present situations."},{"wrong":"If he had studied, he would have passed the exam today.","right":"If he had studied, he would pass the exam today.","note":"For a present result, use 'would' + base verb, not 'would have' + past participle."}],"related":["gp.its-time-and-would-rather","gp.third-conditional","gp.unless-and-as-long-as"]},{"id":"gp.modal-perfects-for-deduction","level":"B2","topicId":"topic.modals","title":"Must have, might have, can't have","summary":"Use 'must have', 'might have', and 'can't have' to make deductions or guesses about things that happened in the past.","explanation":"When we want to make a guess or a deduction about something that happened in the past, we use modal verbs followed by 'have' and the past participle of the main verb.\n\nWe use *must have* + past participle when we are almost certain that something happened. It's a strong logical conclusion based on evidence. For example, if you see wet streets, you might say, \"It *must have rained* last night.\"\n\nWe use *can't have* + past participle when we are almost certain that something *didn't* happen. It expresses a strong belief that something was impossible in the past. For instance, \"They *can't have finished* the race already; it just started!\"\n\nWe use *might have* + past participle when we think something was possible, but we are not sure. It expresses uncertainty or a weaker possibility. For example, \"I don't know where my keys are. I *might have left* them at the office.\"","patterns":["Subject + must have + past participle","Subject + can't have + past participle","Subject + might have + past participle"],"examples":[{"text":"The lights are off. They must have gone out.","note":"Expresses a strong deduction that they left.","source":{"kind":"generated"}},{"text":"She might have forgotten her umbrella.","note":"Indicates a possibility, but not certainty.","source":{"kind":"generated"}},{"text":"He can't have finished the entire book in one hour!","note":"Shows strong certainty that it was impossible for him to finish.","source":{"kind":"generated"}},{"text":"I saw a new bike in their garage. They must have bought it recently.","note":"A logical conclusion based on observation.","source":{"kind":"generated"}},{"text":"The package isn't here. It might have been delivered to the wrong address.","note":"Expresses a possible past event in the passive voice.","source":{"kind":"generated"}},{"text":"Yanni's hands are sticky. He must have been eating figs.","source":{"kind":"tatoeba","sentenceId":9768863,"author":"Amastan"}},{"text":"Tom can't have gone far.","source":{"kind":"tatoeba","sentenceId":1951815,"author":"CK"}}],"commonErrors":[{"wrong":"He must be tired yesterday.","right":"He must have been tired yesterday.","note":"Use 'must have' for deductions about the past."},{"wrong":"They can't know about the surprise party last week.","right":"They can't have known about the surprise party last week.","note":"'Can't have' expresses impossibility in the past."},{"wrong":"She might have went home early.","right":"She might have gone home early.","note":"Always use the past participle after 'have'."}],"related":["gp.had-better","gp.should-have-and-could-have","gp.dont-have-to-vs-mustnt"]},{"id":"gp.non-defining-relative-clauses","level":"B2","topicId":"topic.relative-clauses","title":"Non-defining relative clauses","summary":"Non-defining relative clauses give extra, non-essential information about a noun, always separated by commas.","explanation":"Non-defining relative clauses add *extra information* about a noun in a sentence. This information isn't essential to understand who or what you're talking about; the sentence would still make sense without it. Think of it as a helpful side note.\n\nThese clauses are always separated from the rest of the sentence by *commas*. If the clause is in the middle of a sentence, it will have a comma before and after it. If it's at the end, it will have a comma before it.\n\nWe use relative pronouns like *who* (for people), *which* (for things), *whose* (for possession), *where* (for places), and *when* (for times). Remember, you *cannot* use \"that\" in non-defining relative clauses.","patterns":["Noun, who + verb/subject + verb, ...","Noun, which + verb/subject + verb, ...","Noun, whose + noun + verb, ...","Noun, where + subject + verb, ...","Noun, when + subject + verb, ..."],"examples":[{"text":"My sister, who lives in Berlin, is visiting next month.","note":"Adds extra information about \"my sister\".","source":{"kind":"generated"}},{"text":"The new cafe, which opened last week, serves excellent coffee.","note":"Gives more detail about \"the new cafe\".","source":{"kind":"generated"}},{"text":"Mr. Henderson, whose car broke down, had to take the bus.","note":"Shows possession related to \"Mr. Henderson\".","source":{"kind":"generated"}},{"text":"Paris, where I spent my honeymoon, is a beautiful city.","note":"Provides extra information about \"Paris\" as a place.","source":{"kind":"generated"}},{"text":"Last summer, when we traveled to Italy, was unforgettable.","note":"Adds detail about \"last summer\" as a time.","source":{"kind":"generated"}},{"text":"We're studying English, which is an important subject nowadays.","source":{"kind":"tatoeba","sentenceId":7798131,"author":"AlanF_US"}},{"text":"The ball, which is red, is pretty.","source":{"kind":"tatoeba","sentenceId":6698082,"author":"Eccles17"}}],"commonErrors":[{"wrong":"My friend that works at the library is very helpful.","right":"My friend, who works at the library, is very helpful.","note":"Use \"who\" for people and separate with commas for non-essential information."},{"wrong":"The old house, which is on the hill is for sale.","right":"The old house, which is on the hill, is for sale.","note":"A non-defining clause in the middle of a sentence needs a comma before and after it."},{"wrong":"My new laptop, that I bought yesterday, is very fast.","right":"My new laptop, which I bought yesterday, is very fast.","note":"Do not use \"that\" in non-defining relative clauses."}],"related":["gp.advanced-relative-clauses","gp.defining-relative-clauses"]},{"id":"gp.order-of-adjectives","level":"B2","topicId":"topic.adjectives-adverbs","title":"Order of adjectives","summary":"Learn the common order for adjectives like opinion, size, age, color, origin, and material when describing a noun.","explanation":"When you use more than one adjective to describe a noun, they usually follow a specific order. While it might feel natural to native speakers, there's a general pattern that helps make sentences clear and easy to understand.\n\nThe most common order starts with adjectives that express your *opinion* (like 'beautiful' or 'ugly'), followed by *size* ('large', 'small'), then *age* ('old', 'new'), *color* ('red', 'blue'), *origin* ('French', 'American'), and finally *material* ('wooden', 'silk').\n\nIt's rare to use more than three or four adjectives before a noun, but knowing this order helps you sound more natural and avoid confusion.","patterns":["Opinion + Size + Age + Color + Origin + Material + Noun","Opinion + Size + Noun","Age + Color + Noun","Color + Material + Noun"],"examples":[{"text":"She bought a beautiful small antique silver mirror.","note":"Opinion (beautiful), Size (small), Age (antique), Material (silver) are in order.","source":{"kind":"generated"}},{"text":"They live in a spacious modern brick house.","note":"Opinion (spacious), Age (modern), Material (brick) are correctly ordered.","source":{"kind":"generated"}},{"text":"He wore a comfortable old blue cotton shirt.","note":"Opinion (comfortable), Age (old), Color (blue), Material (cotton) follow the pattern.","source":{"kind":"generated"}},{"text":"We saw a magnificent huge ancient stone temple.","note":"Opinion (magnificent), Size (huge), Age (ancient), Material (stone) are in the correct sequence.","source":{"kind":"generated"}},{"text":"I found a charming little green ceramic mug.","note":"Opinion (charming), Size (little), Color (green), Material (ceramic) are ordered correctly.","source":{"kind":"generated"}},{"text":"A nice big glass of water is a good way to start the day.","source":{"kind":"tatoeba","sentenceId":2819799,"author":"memries"}},{"text":"He's wearing a white cotton shirt.","source":{"kind":"tatoeba","sentenceId":2134063,"author":"Hybrid"}}],"commonErrors":[{"wrong":"She has a red old car.","right":"She has an old red car.","note":"Age (old) comes before Color (red)."},{"wrong":"They bought a wooden big table.","right":"They bought a big wooden table.","note":"Size (big) comes before Material (wooden)."},{"wrong":"He admired the French beautiful painting.","right":"He admired the beautiful French painting.","note":"Opinion (beautiful) comes before Origin (French)."}],"related":["gp.gradable-and-extreme-adjectives","gp.so-and-such","gp.adverbs-of-frequency"]},{"id":"gp.participle-clauses","level":"B2","topicId":"topic.clauses","title":"Participle clauses","summary":"Participle clauses use -ing or -ed forms of verbs to add information about reason, time, or result, making sentences more concise.","explanation":"Participle clauses help combine two sentences or clauses into one, making your writing more concise and fluent. They often replace conjunctions like 'because,' 'when,' 'while,' or 'as a result.' The subject of the participle clause is usually the same as the subject of the main clause.\n\nWe use the *present participle* (-ing form) for active actions or actions happening at the same time as the main verb. For example, \"*Feeling hungry*, I made a sandwich\" means \"Because I felt hungry, I made a sandwich.\"\n\nWe use the *past participle* (-ed form) for passive actions or actions that happened before the main verb. For example, \"*Motivated by the challenge*, she worked harder\" means \"Because she was motivated by the challenge, she worked harder.\" You can also use \"*Having + past participle*\" to emphasize that one action was completed before another, like \"*Having finished her work*, she went home.\"","patterns":["Verb-ing + , + main clause (reason, time, result)","Past participle + , + main clause (reason, time)","Having + past participle + , + main clause (time, reason)","Main clause + , + verb-ing (result)"],"examples":[{"text":"Walking along the beach, they found some interesting shells.","note":"Shows an action happening at the same time (time).","source":{"kind":"generated"}},{"text":"Having completed the project, the team celebrated their success.","note":"Shows an action completed before another (time/reason).","source":{"kind":"generated"}},{"text":"Surprised by the sudden noise, the cat jumped off the sofa.","note":"Explains the reason for the cat's action (reason).","source":{"kind":"generated"}},{"text":"The heavy rain caused flooding, damaging several roads.","note":"Describes the result of the heavy rain (result).","source":{"kind":"generated"}},{"text":"Built in the 18th century, the old house has a lot of history.","note":"Provides information about the house (reason/description).","source":{"kind":"generated"}},{"text":"We walked along the busy street, singing old songs.","source":{"kind":"tatoeba","sentenceId":247774,"author":"CM"}},{"text":"All things considered, she is a fairly good wife.","source":{"kind":"tatoeba","sentenceId":51664,"author":"CM"}}],"commonErrors":[{"wrong":"Running late, the bus was missed.","right":"Running late, I missed the bus.","note":"The subject of the participle clause must be the same as the main clause."},{"wrong":"Having finish the book, she started a new one.","right":"Having finished the book, she started a new one.","note":"Use the past participle form after 'having'."},{"wrong":"Known for its beauty, people visit the park.","right":"Known for its beauty, the park attracts many visitors.","note":"Ensure the participle clause clearly refers to the correct noun."}],"related":["gp.purpose-clauses","gp.as-if-and-as-though","gp.nominalisation"]},{"id":"gp.passive-all-tenses","level":"B2","topicId":"topic.passive","title":"The passive in all tenses","summary":"Learn how to form and use the passive voice in different tenses, including continuous, perfect, and modal forms, to focus on the action or object.","explanation":"The passive voice is used when you want to focus on the action itself or the object that receives the action, rather than the person or thing performing the action. It's especially useful when the doer is unknown, unimportant, or obvious from the context.\n\nTo form the passive voice, you use a form of the verb *to be* followed by the *past participle* of the main verb. The form of *to be* changes depending on the tense you want to use.\n\nFor example, in the present continuous passive, we use 'is/are being + past participle'. For the present perfect passive, it's 'has/have been + past participle'. For the future simple passive, we use 'will be + past participle'. And for modal verbs, it's 'modal + be + past participle'. If you need to mention who performed the action, you can use 'by + agent' at the end of the sentence.","patterns":["is/are being + past participle","has/have been + past participle","will be + past participle","modal + be + past participle"],"examples":[{"text":"The new city park is being designed by a famous architect.","note":"Present continuous passive: describes an action in progress now.","source":{"kind":"generated"}},{"text":"Many new features have been added to the app recently.","note":"Present perfect passive: describes a completed action with a result relevant now.","source":{"kind":"generated"}},{"text":"The annual conference will be held in Paris next year.","note":"Future simple passive: describes an action planned for the future.","source":{"kind":"generated"}},{"text":"This ancient artifact must be handled with extreme care.","note":"Modal passive (obligation): expresses necessity.","source":{"kind":"generated"}},{"text":"Important decisions can be made more quickly with this new system.","note":"Modal passive (ability): expresses possibility or capability.","source":{"kind":"generated"}},{"text":"Next year's conference will be held in Australia.","source":{"kind":"tatoeba","sentenceId":7152908,"author":"CK"}},{"text":"The festival will be held next week.","source":{"kind":"tatoeba","sentenceId":49961,"author":"CK"}}],"commonErrors":[{"wrong":"The report is finish by Friday.","right":"The report will be finished by Friday.","note":"Remember to use the correct form of 'be' and the past participle for the future passive."},{"wrong":"The new software has install.","right":"The new software has been installed.","note":"The present perfect passive requires 'has/have been' + past participle."},{"wrong":"The documents are being prepare.","right":"The documents are being prepared.","note":"Always use the past participle form of the main verb."}],"related":["gp.causative-have-and-get","gp.reporting-passive","gp.advanced-passive-forms"]},{"id":"gp.purpose-clauses","level":"B2","topicId":"topic.clauses","title":"So that and in order to","summary":"Learn to use 'so that' and 'in order to' to explain the purpose or reason behind an action.","explanation":"We use 'so that' and 'in order to' to explain *why* someone does something. They both introduce the purpose or intention of an action.\n\n'In order to' is followed by the base form of a verb. It is generally more formal than 'so that' and is often used in written English or formal speech. You can also use 'to' + base verb for the same purpose, which is less formal than 'in order to'.\n\n'So that' is followed by a clause (a subject and a verb). We often use a modal verb like 'can', 'could', 'will', or 'would' after 'so that' to express possibility or intention. For example, 'She studies hard so that she *can* pass the exam.'","patterns":["Subject + verb + in order to + base verb","Subject + verb + so that + subject + modal verb + base verb"],"examples":[{"text":"He woke up early in order to catch the first train.","note":"Explains the purpose of waking up early.","source":{"kind":"generated"}},{"text":"She wears a hat so that she doesn't get sunburned.","note":"Shows the reason for wearing a hat, using 'so that' with a negative clause.","source":{"kind":"generated"}},{"text":"They saved money in order to buy a new apartment.","note":"States the goal of saving money.","source":{"kind":"generated"}},{"text":"I'll send you the report so that you can review it before the meeting.","note":"Indicates the intention behind sending the report, using 'can'.","source":{"kind":"generated"}},{"text":"We left a note on the door so that the delivery driver would know we were out.","note":"Explains the reason for leaving a note, using 'would'.","source":{"kind":"generated"}},{"text":"I'm breathing so that I can calm down.","source":{"kind":"tatoeba","sentenceId":11579137,"author":"Amastan"}},{"text":"Plants need warmth and light in order to grow.","source":{"kind":"tatoeba","sentenceId":12283831,"author":"Lebensfreude"}}],"commonErrors":[{"wrong":"I study hard so I pass the test.","right":"I study hard so that I can pass the test.","note":"Remember to include 'that' and a modal verb after 'so' when expressing purpose."},{"wrong":"She works extra hours in order to she earns more money.","right":"She works extra hours in order to earn more money.","note":"After 'in order to', use the base form of the verb, not a full clause."},{"wrong":"He bought a map for not get lost.","right":"He bought a map in order not to get lost.","note":"For negative purpose with 'in order to', place 'not' before 'to'."}],"related":["gp.participle-clauses","gp.as-if-and-as-though","gp.nominalisation"]},{"id":"gp.remember-stop-try-forget","level":"B2","topicId":"topic.verb-patterns","title":"Verbs that change meaning","summary":"Learn how verbs like 'remember,' 'stop,' 'try,' and 'forget' change meaning when followed by an -ing form or a to-infinitive.","explanation":"Some verbs in English can be followed by either an *-ing* form (gerund) or a *to-infinitive*, but the choice changes the meaning of the sentence. It's important to understand these differences to express yourself accurately.\n\nFor example, *remember to do something* means you recall a task you need to complete. *Remember doing something* means you have a memory of a past event. Similarly, *forget to do something* means you didn't do a task, while *forget doing something* means you don't recall a past action.\n\nWith 'stop,' *stop to do something* means you pause one activity to start another. *Stop doing something* means you finish or cease an activity completely. Finally, *try to do something* means you make an effort to achieve something, often difficult. *Try doing something* means you experiment with an action to see what happens or if it helps.","patterns":["remember + to + verb (task)","remember + verb-ing (memory)","stop + to + verb (purpose)","stop + verb-ing (cease activity)","try + to + verb (effort)","try + verb-ing (experiment)","forget + to + verb (missed task)","forget + verb-ing (no memory)"],"examples":[{"text":"Did you remember to lock the door before you left?","note":"Recalling a necessary action for the future.","source":{"kind":"generated"}},{"text":"I remember visiting that museum when I was a child.","note":"Recalling a past experience or memory.","source":{"kind":"generated"}},{"text":"We stopped to admire the beautiful sunset.","note":"Pausing one activity (traveling) to do another (admire).","source":{"kind":"generated"}},{"text":"He stopped smoking five years ago.","note":"Ceasing the activity of smoking completely.","source":{"kind":"generated"}},{"text":"I tried calling her, but her phone was off.","note":"Experimenting with an action (calling) to see the result.","source":{"kind":"generated"}},{"text":"I wanted Tom to stop eating so much junk food.","source":{"kind":"tatoeba","sentenceId":8426963,"author":"CK"}},{"text":"Did you really forget to take your medicine this morning?","source":{"kind":"tatoeba","sentenceId":6062412,"author":"CK"}}],"commonErrors":[{"wrong":"I stopped smoking to take a break.","right":"I stopped to smoke to take a break.","note":"Use 'to smoke' to show the purpose of stopping."},{"wrong":"Don't forget buying milk on your way home.","right":"Don't forget to buy milk on your way home.","note":"Use 'to buy' for a task you need to remember for the future."},{"wrong":"She tried opening the jar, but it was too tight.","right":"She tried to open the jar, but it was too tight.","note":"Use 'to open' to describe making an effort to do something difficult."}],"related":["gp.be-used-to-and-get-used-to","gp.gerund-or-infinitive","gp.phrasal-verbs-basics"]},{"id":"gp.reporting-passive","level":"B2","topicId":"topic.passive","title":"It is said that, he is thought to","summary":"Use passive structures like 'It is said that' or 'He is thought to' to report general beliefs, claims, or opinions without stating who said them.","explanation":"We often use passive reporting verbs to talk about what people generally believe, say, or know. This structure allows us to share information or opinions without needing to name the specific person or group who holds that belief.\n\nThere are two main ways to form these sentences. The first uses \"It is + reporting verb (past participle) + that + clause.\" This is a common way to introduce a general statement or fact. The second way uses \"Subject + is/are + reporting verb (past participle) + to + infinitive.\" This structure focuses on a specific person or thing that is the subject of the belief or claim.\n\nCommon reporting verbs used in this way include *say*, *think*, *believe*, *know*, *report*, *understand*, and *expect*. When the reported action happened before the reporting verb, we use the perfect infinitive: \"to have + past participle.\"","patterns":["It is + said/thought/believed + that + clause","Subject + is/are + said/thought/believed + to + infinitive (base form)","Subject + is/are + said/thought/believed + to + have + past participle (perfect infinitive)"],"examples":[{"text":"It is believed that the new restaurant will open next month.","note":"Reports a general belief about a future event.","source":{"kind":"generated"}},{"text":"The ancient ruins are said to be over a thousand years old.","note":"Reports a general claim about the age of the ruins.","source":{"kind":"generated"}},{"text":"She is thought to have traveled extensively in her youth.","note":"Reports a belief about a past action using the perfect infinitive.","source":{"kind":"generated"}},{"text":"It is understood that the project will require more funding.","note":"Reports a general understanding or expectation.","source":{"kind":"generated"}},{"text":"The new policy is expected to improve efficiency.","note":"Reports an expectation about the policy's effect.","source":{"kind":"generated"}},{"text":"His new novel is said to be based on personal experience.","source":{"kind":"tatoeba","sentenceId":8276225,"author":"sundown"}},{"text":"It is said that he knows the secret.","source":{"kind":"tatoeba","sentenceId":291242,"author":"CM"}}],"commonErrors":[{"wrong":"People say that he is a great leader.","right":"It is said that he is a great leader.","note":"Use the passive 'It is said that' for general reports."},{"wrong":"He is believed be a talented musician.","right":"He is believed to be a talented musician.","note":"Remember to include 'to' before the infinitive."},{"wrong":"It is thought him to be very intelligent.","right":"He is thought to be very intelligent.","note":"The subject of the reported action becomes the subject of the passive verb."}],"related":["gp.causative-have-and-get","gp.passive-all-tenses","gp.advanced-passive-forms"]},{"id":"gp.reporting-verbs","level":"B2","topicId":"topic.reported-speech","title":"Reporting verbs","summary":"Learn how to use specific reporting verbs like 'suggest', 'deny', 'admit', 'warn', and 'refuse' with their correct grammatical patterns.","explanation":"When we report what someone said, we often use verbs like 'say' or 'tell'. However, there are many other reporting verbs that give more detail about the speaker's intention or the type of message. These verbs help us understand if someone was making a suggestion, denying a fact, admitting a mistake, giving a warning, or refusing to do something.\n\nEach of these specific reporting verbs is followed by a particular grammatical structure. For example, some verbs are followed by a gerund (verb-ing), others by a 'that' clause, and some by an infinitive (to + verb). Knowing these patterns is key to using them correctly.\n\nUsing these verbs makes your reported speech more precise and natural. Instead of just saying \"He said he didn't do it,\" you can say \"He *denied doing* it,\" which is more concise and informative.","patterns":["suggest + verb-ing","suggest + that + clause (with or without 'should')","deny + verb-ing","deny + that + clause","admit + verb-ing","admit + that + clause","warn + (someone) + (not) to-infinitive","warn + (someone) + about + noun/verb-ing","warn + that + clause","refuse + to-infinitive"],"examples":[{"text":"My colleague *suggested trying* the new coffee shop.","note":"Uses 'suggest' followed by a gerund (verb-ing).","source":{"kind":"generated"}},{"text":"The suspect *denied that* he was at the scene of the crime.","note":"Uses 'deny' followed by a 'that' clause.","source":{"kind":"generated"}},{"text":"After a long discussion, she *admitted making* a mistake.","note":"Uses 'admit' followed by a gerund (verb-ing).","source":{"kind":"generated"}},{"text":"The park ranger *warned us not to feed* the wild animals.","note":"Uses 'warn' with an object and a negative to-infinitive.","source":{"kind":"generated"}},{"text":"He *refused to answer* any more questions from the reporters.","note":"Uses 'refuse' followed by a to-infinitive.","source":{"kind":"generated"}},{"text":"Tom refused to sign the contract.","source":{"kind":"tatoeba","sentenceId":2642083,"author":"CK"}},{"text":"I suggested that John be called.","source":{"kind":"tatoeba","sentenceId":253936,"author":"Dejo"}}],"commonErrors":[{"wrong":"She suggested to go to the beach.","right":"She suggested going to the beach.","note":"'Suggest' is followed by a gerund (verb-ing), not a to-infinitive."},{"wrong":"They warned about to be careful.","right":"They warned us to be careful.","note":"'Warn' often takes an object before the to-infinitive, or 'about' with a noun/gerund."},{"wrong":"I denied to know anything about it.","right":"I denied knowing anything about it.","note":"'Deny' is followed by a gerund (verb-ing), not a to-infinitive."}],"related":["gp.reported-questions","gp.reported-statements","gp.nuanced-reporting-verbs"]},{"id":"gp.should-have-and-could-have","level":"B2","topicId":"topic.modals","title":"Should have and could have","summary":"Use 'should have' to express regret or criticism about past actions, and 'could have' for missed opportunities or possibilities that didn't happen.","explanation":"We use *should have* + past participle to talk about things that didn't happen in the past, but we think they were a good idea or the right thing to do. It often expresses regret about our own actions or criticism of someone else's actions.\n\nWe use *could have* + past participle to talk about something that was possible in the past but didn't happen. It suggests a missed opportunity or an alternative action that was available but not taken.\n\nBoth structures refer to hypothetical situations in the past, meaning they describe what might have happened or what would have been better, but did not actually occur.","patterns":["Subject + should have + past participle","Subject + could have + past participle","Subject + shouldn't have + past participle","Subject + couldn't have + past participle"],"examples":[{"text":"I should have studied harder for the exam.","note":"Expresses regret about a past action (not studying enough).","source":{"kind":"generated"}},{"text":"They shouldn't have left the door unlocked.","note":"Shows criticism of a past action (leaving the door unlocked).","source":{"kind":"generated"}},{"text":"We could have taken the earlier train.","note":"Indicates a missed opportunity or alternative possibility in the past.","source":{"kind":"generated"}},{"text":"She could have helped us, but she chose not to.","note":"Suggests a past ability or option that was not used.","source":{"kind":"generated"}},{"text":"He couldn't have known about the surprise party.","note":"Expresses a past impossibility or a strong belief that something wasn't possible.","source":{"kind":"generated"}},{"text":"I'm sorry. I should have been more careful.","source":{"kind":"tatoeba","sentenceId":6735978,"author":"Hybrid"}},{"text":"You could have shared your feelings with me.","source":{"kind":"tatoeba","sentenceId":10742893,"author":"Danika_Dakika"}}],"commonErrors":[{"wrong":"I should studied more.","right":"I should have studied more.","note":"Remember to use 'have' after 'should' or 'could'."},{"wrong":"They could of gone to the concert.","right":"They could have gone to the concert.","note":"The correct auxiliary verb is 'have', not 'of'."},{"wrong":"She should has called me.","right":"She should have called me.","note":"Always use 'have' (not 'has') after 'should' or 'could', regardless of the subject."}],"related":["gp.had-better","gp.modal-perfects-for-deduction","gp.dont-have-to-vs-mustnt"]},{"id":"gp.the-more-the-better","level":"B2","topicId":"topic.comparison","title":"The more ..., the better","summary":"Use \"The more..., the more...\" or \"The more..., the less...\" to show how two things change together proportionally.","explanation":"This structure is used to show that one thing depends on another. As one thing increases or decreases, the other thing also increases or decreases in proportion. It's a way to express cause and effect concisely.\n\nWe use \"the\" before each comparative adjective or adverb. It's like saying \"to the extent that one thing changes, to that same extent the other thing changes.\" For example, \"The more you read, the more you learn.\"\n\nYou can use \"more\" or \"less\" with longer adjectives and adverbs (e.g., *the more difficult*, *the less interesting*), or the \"-er\" ending for shorter ones (e.g., *the faster*, *the colder*).","patterns":["The + comparative adjective/adverb, the + comparative adjective/adverb","The more + noun, the more + noun","The less + noun, the less + noun"],"examples":[{"text":"The warmer the weather gets, the happier I feel.","note":"Shows a direct relationship between temperature and happiness.","source":{"kind":"generated"}},{"text":"The more you practice, the better you become.","note":"Explains that improvement comes with effort.","source":{"kind":"generated"}},{"text":"The less sugar you eat, the healthier you'll be.","note":"Indicates a proportional decrease in sugar leads to better health.","source":{"kind":"generated"}},{"text":"The faster the car goes, the more fuel it uses.","note":"Describes how speed affects fuel consumption.","source":{"kind":"generated"}},{"text":"The more books I read, the more I want to learn.","note":"Expresses an increasing desire for knowledge with more reading.","source":{"kind":"generated"}},{"text":"The less I know about Tom, the better.","source":{"kind":"tatoeba","sentenceId":10282388,"author":"ddnktr"}},{"text":"The more you know about something, the more interesting it becomes.","source":{"kind":"tatoeba","sentenceId":11864708,"author":"CK"}}],"commonErrors":[{"wrong":"More you study, more you learn.","right":"The more you study, the more you learn.","note":"Always use \"the\" before each comparative."},{"wrong":"The most I eat, the fatter I get.","right":"The more I eat, the fatter I get.","note":"Use the comparative form \"more,\" not the superlative \"most.\""},{"wrong":"The more quickly you finish, the soon you can leave.","right":"The more quickly you finish, the sooner you can leave.","note":"Both parts of the structure need to be in the comparative form."}],"related":["gp.as-as-comparisons","gp.comparative-adjectives","gp.superlative-adjectives"]},{"id":"gp.third-conditional","level":"B2","topicId":"topic.conditionals","title":"Third conditional","summary":"The third conditional describes imagined past situations and their hypothetical results, often expressing regret or speculation.","explanation":"We use the third conditional to talk about things that *didn't happen* in the past and their *imagined results*. It describes a hypothetical situation in the past and what *would have happened* if that situation had been different.\n\nThis structure often expresses regret, criticism, or speculation about past events. It helps us consider alternative outcomes for past actions or circumstances.\n\nThe 'if' clause uses the past perfect (had + past participle), and the main clause uses 'would have' + past participle. The order of the clauses can be reversed without changing the meaning.","patterns":["If + subject + had + past participle, subject + would have + past participle.","Subject + would have + past participle + if + subject + had + past participle."],"examples":[{"text":"If I had known about the sale, I would have bought a new laptop.","note":"Imagined past knowledge (I didn't know), imagined past action (I didn't buy).","source":{"kind":"generated"}},{"text":"They wouldn't have missed their flight if they had left for the airport earlier.","note":"Imagined past result (they missed it), imagined past action (they left late).","source":{"kind":"generated"}},{"text":"If she had studied more, she would have passed the difficult exam.","note":"Imagined past action (she didn't study), imagined past result (she failed).","source":{"kind":"generated"}},{"text":"We would have gone to the concert if we had found tickets.","note":"Imagined past action (we didn't go), imagined past condition (we didn't find tickets).","source":{"kind":"generated"}},{"text":"If the weather hadn't been so bad, we would have had a picnic.","note":"Imagined past condition (the weather was bad), imagined past result (we didn't have a picnic).","source":{"kind":"generated"}},{"text":"If he had come five minutes later, he would have missed the train.","source":{"kind":"tatoeba","sentenceId":31689,"author":"CK"}},{"text":"If you had studied harder, you would have passed the examination.","source":{"kind":"tatoeba","sentenceId":30468,"author":"CK"}}],"commonErrors":[{"wrong":"If I would have known, I would have helped.","right":"If I had known, I would have helped.","note":"Use the past perfect (had + past participle) in the 'if' clause."},{"wrong":"If she had called, I would answer.","right":"If she had called, I would have answered.","note":"Use 'would have' + past participle in the main clause."},{"wrong":"If they had went to the party, they would have met him.","right":"If they had gone to the party, they would have met him.","note":"Ensure the correct past participle form of the verb is used."}],"related":["gp.its-time-and-would-rather","gp.mixed-conditionals","gp.unless-and-as-long-as"]},{"id":"gp.unless-and-as-long-as","level":"B2","topicId":"topic.conditionals","title":"Unless, as long as, provided that","summary":"Learn to use 'unless,' 'as long as,' and 'provided that' to express conditions without using 'if.'","explanation":"“Unless,” “as long as,” and “provided that” are conjunctions that introduce conditions, much like “if.” They help you specify what must happen for another event or situation to be true or possible.\n\n*Unless* means “if not.” It introduces a negative condition, indicating that something will happen only if the condition is *not* met. For example, “Unless you hurry, you’ll miss the train” means “If you don’t hurry, you’ll miss the train.”\n\n*As long as* and *provided that* (or *providing that*) introduce a strong, necessary condition. They mean “only if” or “on the condition that.” These phrases emphasize that the main action will only occur if the specific condition is fulfilled. For instance, “You can use my laptop as long as you’re careful” sets a clear requirement for using the laptop.","patterns":["Unless + subject + verb, subject + will/won't + verb","Subject + will/won't + verb + unless + subject + verb","As long as + subject + verb, subject + will/can + verb","Subject + will/can + verb + as long as + subject + verb","Provided that + subject + verb, subject + will/can + verb"],"examples":[{"text":"Unless it rains, we'll go hiking this afternoon.","note":"This means 'If it doesn't rain, we'll go hiking.'","source":{"kind":"generated"}},{"text":"You can join the club as long as you attend the weekly meetings.","note":"This sets a necessary condition for joining the club.","source":{"kind":"generated"}},{"text":"Provided that the flight isn't delayed, we'll arrive on time.","note":"This states a condition for arriving on time, often more formal.","source":{"kind":"generated"}},{"text":"I won't buy a new car unless I get a promotion at work.","note":"The promotion is the only condition under which a new car will be bought.","source":{"kind":"generated"}},{"text":"We can finish the project by Friday, providing that everyone works extra hours.","note":"This uses 'providing that' to state a strong requirement.","source":{"kind":"generated"}},{"text":"Don't worry about how much it costs, as long as it's reasonable.","source":{"kind":"tatoeba","sentenceId":3098894,"author":"CK"}},{"text":"I won't go unless Tom goes with me.","source":{"kind":"tatoeba","sentenceId":3444549,"author":"CK"}}],"commonErrors":[{"wrong":"Unless you don't study, you will fail the exam.","right":"Unless you study, you will fail the exam.","note":"Unless already includes 'not', so don't use another negative."},{"wrong":"I will lend you the book, provided you return it back soon.","right":"I will lend you the book, provided that you return it soon.","note":"Use 'provided that' or 'providing that' for the full phrase."},{"wrong":"As long as you are careful, you can drive my car.","right":"As long as you are careful, you can drive my car.","note":"The condition comes first, then the result."}],"related":["gp.its-time-and-would-rather","gp.mixed-conditionals","gp.third-conditional"]},{"id":"gp.wish-and-if-only","level":"B2","topicId":"topic.conditionals","title":"Wish and if only","summary":"Use 'wish' and 'if only' to express desires for things to be different in the present or future, or regrets about the past.","explanation":"We use 'wish' and 'if only' to talk about situations we want to change. These situations are usually not true or not possible at the moment, or they are things we regret from the past. 'If only' is often stronger and more dramatic than 'wish'.\n\nTo express a wish about the *present* or *future*, we use 'wish' or 'if only' followed by the *past simple* tense. This shows that we want a current situation to be different. For example, if you don't have a car, you might say, \"I wish I had a car.\"\n\nTo express a *regret about the past*, we use 'wish' or 'if only' followed by the *past perfect* tense. This shows that we are sorry about something that happened or didn't happen. For example, \"I wish I had studied harder for the exam.\"\n\nWe can also use 'wish' or 'if only' with *would* to talk about things we want other people or situations to change, especially if we are annoyed. For example, \"I wish the bus would arrive soon.\" We generally don't use 'would' to talk about our own actions.","patterns":["wish/if only + subject + past simple (for present/future wishes)","wish/if only + subject + past perfect (for past regrets)","wish/if only + subject + would + base verb (for other people's behavior/situations)"],"examples":[{"text":"I wish I spoke French fluently.","note":"Expresses a wish about a present ability.","source":{"kind":"generated"}},{"text":"If only it weren't raining right now!","note":"Expresses a strong wish for a present situation to be different.","source":{"kind":"generated"}},{"text":"She wishes she had saved more money last year.","note":"Expresses regret about a past action.","source":{"kind":"generated"}},{"text":"They wish their neighbors would turn down the music.","note":"Expresses a desire for someone else's behavior to change.","source":{"kind":"generated"}},{"text":"I wish I hadn't forgotten my umbrella this morning.","note":"Expresses regret about a past event.","source":{"kind":"generated"}},{"text":"I wish I hadn't agreed to do that.","source":{"kind":"tatoeba","sentenceId":6347917,"author":"CK"}},{"text":"I wish I had more energy.","source":{"kind":"tatoeba","sentenceId":5156211,"author":"CK"}}],"commonErrors":[{"wrong":"I wish I am taller.","right":"I wish I were taller.","note":"Use the past simple (or 'were' for 'be') for present wishes."},{"wrong":"If only I didn't eat so much cake yesterday.","right":"If only I hadn't eaten so much cake yesterday.","note":"Use the past perfect for regrets about the past."},{"wrong":"He wishes he would have more free time.","right":"He wishes he had more free time.","note":"Don't use 'would' for wishes about your own present situation."}],"related":["gp.its-time-and-would-rather","gp.mixed-conditionals","gp.third-conditional"]},{"id":"gp.would-for-past-habits","level":"B2","topicId":"topic.tenses","title":"Would for past habits","summary":"Learn to use 'would' for repeated actions in the past, and how it differs from 'used to' for past habits and states.","explanation":"We use *would* to talk about actions that we repeated regularly in the past. It describes habits or routines that are no longer true. It often appears with time expressions like 'every day,' 'on weekends,' or 'when I was younger.'\n\nFor example, 'When I was a child, I *would* play outside every day.' This means playing outside was a regular activity. It emphasizes the routine nature of the action.\n\n*Would* is similar to *used to* for past habits. However, *would* can only describe *actions*, not *states*. For past states (like 'be,' 'have,' 'live,' 'know,' 'like'), you must use *used to*. For instance, you can say 'I *used to* live in a small town,' but not 'I *would* live in a small town.'","patterns":["Subject + would + base verb","Subject + wouldn't + base verb"],"examples":[{"text":"Every summer, my family would visit the beach for a week.","note":"Describes a repeated action (visiting) in the past.","source":{"kind":"generated"}},{"text":"When I was in college, I would study late into the night.","note":"Shows a regular habit (studying) from a past period.","source":{"kind":"generated"}},{"text":"My grandmother wouldn't let us eat dessert before dinner.","note":"Indicates a repeated refusal or rule in the past.","source":{"kind":"generated"}},{"text":"Before the new cafe opened, we would always meet at the old one.","note":"Refers to a past routine (meeting) that has changed.","source":{"kind":"generated"}},{"text":"On rainy days, my brother and I would read books for hours.","note":"Highlights a repeated action (reading) that happened under specific conditions.","source":{"kind":"generated"}}],"commonErrors":[{"wrong":"I would be very shy when I was younger.","right":"I used to be very shy when I was younger.","note":"'Would' cannot be used for past states like 'be'."},{"wrong":"My old dog would barked at every squirrel.","right":"My old dog would bark at every squirrel.","note":"After 'would', use the base form of the verb."},{"wrong":"They would had a big garden in their previous house.","right":"They used to have a big garden in their previous house.","note":"'Would' cannot be used for past states like 'have'."}],"related":["gp.past-perfect","gp.present-perfect-continuous","gp.present-perfect-for-and-since"]}],"lessons":[{"id":"lesson.b2.articles-in-general-statements","level":"B2","topicId":"topic.articles-determiners","title":"Articles in general statements","goal":"Learn when to use 'the' or no article at all when talking about things in general, including institutions like school or hospital.","minutes":10,"grammarPointIds":["gp.articles-in-general-statements"],"soundIds":[],"exerciseIds":["ex.b2.articles-in-general-statements.01","ex.b2.articles-in-general-statements.02","ex.b2.articles-in-general-statements.03","ex.b2.articles-in-general-statements.04","ex.b2.articles-in-general-statements.05","ex.b2.articles-in-general-statements.06","ex.b2.articles-in-general-statements.08","ex.b2.articles-in-general-statements.10","ex.b2.articles-in-general-statements.11","ex.b2.articles-in-general-statements.12","ex.b2.articles-in-general-statements.13","ex.b2.articles-in-general-statements.14"]},{"id":"lesson.b2.be-used-to-and-get-used-to","level":"B2","topicId":"topic.verb-patterns","title":"Be used to and get used to","goal":"Learn to talk about being familiar with something or becoming familiar with it using 'be used to' and 'get used to'.","minutes":12,"grammarPointIds":["gp.be-used-to-and-get-used-to"],"soundIds":[],"exerciseIds":["ex.b2.be-used-to-and-get-used-to.01","ex.b2.be-used-to-and-get-used-to.02","ex.b2.be-used-to-and-get-used-to.03","ex.b2.be-used-to-and-get-used-to.04","ex.b2.be-used-to-and-get-used-to.08","ex.b2.be-used-to-and-get-used-to.09","ex.b2.be-used-to-and-get-used-to.10","ex.b2.be-used-to-and-get-used-to.11","ex.b2.be-used-to-and-get-used-to.12","ex.b2.be-used-to-and-get-used-to.13","ex.b2.be-used-to-and-get-used-to.14","ex.b2.be-used-to-and-get-used-to.15","ex.b2.be-used-to-and-get-used-to.16","ex.b2.be-used-to-and-get-used-to.17","ex.b2.be-used-to-and-get-used-to.18"]},{"id":"lesson.b2.causative-have-and-get","level":"B2","topicId":"topic.passive","title":"Have and get something done","goal":"Learn to use 'have something done' and 'get something done' to talk about arranging for someone else to perform a service for you.","minutes":11,"grammarPointIds":["gp.causative-have-and-get"],"soundIds":[],"exerciseIds":["ex.b2.causative-have-and-get.01","ex.b2.causative-have-and-get.02","ex.b2.causative-have-and-get.03","ex.b2.causative-have-and-get.04","ex.b2.causative-have-and-get.05","ex.b2.causative-have-and-get.06","ex.b2.causative-have-and-get.07","ex.b2.causative-have-and-get.08","ex.b2.causative-have-and-get.09","ex.b2.causative-have-and-get.10","ex.b2.causative-have-and-get.11","ex.b2.causative-have-and-get.12","ex.b2.causative-have-and-get.13","ex.b2.causative-have-and-get.14"]},{"id":"lesson.b2.despite-and-in-spite-of","level":"B2","topicId":"topic.linking","title":"Despite, in spite of and whereas","goal":"Use \"despite,\" \"in spite of,\" and \"whereas\" to show contrast between ideas or facts, whether surprising or direct.","minutes":12,"grammarPointIds":["gp.despite-and-in-spite-of"],"soundIds":[],"exerciseIds":["ex.b2.despite-and-in-spite-of.01","ex.b2.despite-and-in-spite-of.02","ex.b2.despite-and-in-spite-of.03","ex.b2.despite-and-in-spite-of.04","ex.b2.despite-and-in-spite-of.06","ex.b2.despite-and-in-spite-of.08","ex.b2.despite-and-in-spite-of.10","ex.b2.despite-and-in-spite-of.11","ex.b2.despite-and-in-spite-of.12","ex.b2.despite-and-in-spite-of.13","ex.b2.despite-and-in-spite-of.14","ex.b2.despite-and-in-spite-of.15","ex.b2.despite-and-in-spite-of.16","ex.b2.despite-and-in-spite-of.17","ex.b2.despite-and-in-spite-of.18"]},{"id":"lesson.b2.each-every-all","level":"B2","topicId":"topic.articles-determiners","title":"Each, every and all","goal":"Learn to use 'each', 'every', and 'all' to talk about all members of a group, focusing on individual items or the group as a whole.","minutes":10,"grammarPointIds":["gp.each-every-all"],"soundIds":[],"exerciseIds":["ex.b2.each-every-all.01","ex.b2.each-every-all.02","ex.b2.each-every-all.03","ex.b2.each-every-all.04","ex.b2.each-every-all.07","ex.b2.each-every-all.08","ex.b2.each-every-all.09","ex.b2.each-every-all.10","ex.b2.each-every-all.11","ex.b2.each-every-all.12","ex.b2.each-every-all.13","ex.b2.each-every-all.14"]},{"id":"lesson.b2.emphatic-do","level":"B2","topicId":"topic.emphasis","title":"Emphatic do","goal":"Use 'do', 'does', or 'did' before a main verb to add emphasis, confirming that something is true, often in response to doubt or a contradiction.","minutes":11,"grammarPointIds":["gp.emphatic-do"],"soundIds":[],"exerciseIds":["ex.b2.emphatic-do.01","ex.b2.emphatic-do.02","ex.b2.emphatic-do.03","ex.b2.emphatic-do.04","ex.b2.emphatic-do.05","ex.b2.emphatic-do.06","ex.b2.emphatic-do.07","ex.b2.emphatic-do.08","ex.b2.emphatic-do.09","ex.b2.emphatic-do.10","ex.b2.emphatic-do.11","ex.b2.emphatic-do.12","ex.b2.emphatic-do.13","ex.b2.emphatic-do.14"]},{"id":"lesson.b2.future-continuous","level":"B2","topicId":"topic.future","title":"Future continuous","goal":"Use the future continuous to talk about actions that will be in progress at a specific time or over a period in the future.","minutes":10,"grammarPointIds":["gp.future-continuous"],"soundIds":[],"exerciseIds":["ex.b2.future-continuous.01","ex.b2.future-continuous.02","ex.b2.future-continuous.03","ex.b2.future-continuous.04","ex.b2.future-continuous.07","ex.b2.future-continuous.08","ex.b2.future-continuous.09","ex.b2.future-continuous.10","ex.b2.future-continuous.11","ex.b2.future-continuous.12","ex.b2.future-continuous.13","ex.b2.future-continuous.14"]},{"id":"lesson.b2.future-perfect","level":"B2","topicId":"topic.future","title":"Future perfect","goal":"Use the future perfect to talk about actions that will be completed before a specific time or event in the future.","minutes":13,"grammarPointIds":["gp.future-perfect"],"soundIds":[],"exerciseIds":["ex.b2.future-perfect.01","ex.b2.future-perfect.02","ex.b2.future-perfect.03","ex.b2.future-perfect.04","ex.b2.future-perfect.06","ex.b2.future-perfect.08","ex.b2.future-perfect.09","ex.b2.future-perfect.10","ex.b2.future-perfect.11","ex.b2.future-perfect.12","ex.b2.future-perfect.13","ex.b2.future-perfect.14","ex.b2.future-perfect.15","ex.b2.future-perfect.16","ex.b2.future-perfect.17","ex.b2.future-perfect.18"]},{"id":"lesson.b2.gradable-and-extreme-adjectives","level":"B2","topicId":"topic.adjectives-adverbs","title":"Very or absolutely","goal":"Learn to use 'very' with gradable adjectives and 'absolutely' with extreme adjectives to intensify their meaning.","minutes":11,"grammarPointIds":["gp.gradable-and-extreme-adjectives"],"soundIds":[],"exerciseIds":["ex.b2.gradable-and-extreme-adjectives.01","ex.b2.gradable-and-extreme-adjectives.02","ex.b2.gradable-and-extreme-adjectives.03","ex.b2.gradable-and-extreme-adjectives.04","ex.b2.gradable-and-extreme-adjectives.05","ex.b2.gradable-and-extreme-adjectives.06","ex.b2.gradable-and-extreme-adjectives.07","ex.b2.gradable-and-extreme-adjectives.08","ex.b2.gradable-and-extreme-adjectives.09","ex.b2.gradable-and-extreme-adjectives.10","ex.b2.gradable-and-extreme-adjectives.11","ex.b2.gradable-and-extreme-adjectives.12","ex.b2.gradable-and-extreme-adjectives.13","ex.b2.gradable-and-extreme-adjectives.14"]},{"id":"lesson.b2.had-better","level":"B2","topicId":"topic.modals","title":"Had better","goal":"Use 'had better' to give strong advice or warnings, often implying a negative consequence if the advice is not followed.","minutes":11,"grammarPointIds":["gp.had-better"],"soundIds":[],"exerciseIds":["ex.b2.had-better.01","ex.b2.had-better.02","ex.b2.had-better.03","ex.b2.had-better.04","ex.b2.had-better.05","ex.b2.had-better.06","ex.b2.had-better.07","ex.b2.had-better.08","ex.b2.had-better.09","ex.b2.had-better.10","ex.b2.had-better.11","ex.b2.had-better.12","ex.b2.had-better.13","ex.b2.had-better.14"]},{"id":"lesson.b2.it-cleft-sentences","level":"B2","topicId":"topic.emphasis","title":"It-cleft sentences","goal":"It-cleft sentences emphasize a specific part of a sentence, like a person, thing, time, or reason, using the structure 'It + be + emphasized part + relative clause'.","minutes":11,"grammarPointIds":["gp.it-cleft-sentences"],"soundIds":[],"exerciseIds":["ex.b2.it-cleft-sentences.01","ex.b2.it-cleft-sentences.02","ex.b2.it-cleft-sentences.03","ex.b2.it-cleft-sentences.04","ex.b2.it-cleft-sentences.05","ex.b2.it-cleft-sentences.06","ex.b2.it-cleft-sentences.07","ex.b2.it-cleft-sentences.08","ex.b2.it-cleft-sentences.09","ex.b2.it-cleft-sentences.10","ex.b2.it-cleft-sentences.11","ex.b2.it-cleft-sentences.12","ex.b2.it-cleft-sentences.13","ex.b2.it-cleft-sentences.14"]},{"id":"lesson.b2.its-time-and-would-rather","level":"B2","topicId":"topic.conditionals","title":"It's time, would rather","goal":"Use 'it's time' and 'would rather' with past tense forms to express that something should happen now, or to state a preference for the present or future.","minutes":11,"grammarPointIds":["gp.its-time-and-would-rather"],"soundIds":[],"exerciseIds":["ex.b2.its-time-and-would-rather.01","ex.b2.its-time-and-would-rather.02","ex.b2.its-time-and-would-rather.03","ex.b2.its-time-and-would-rather.04","ex.b2.its-time-and-would-rather.05","ex.b2.its-time-and-would-rather.06","ex.b2.its-time-and-would-rather.07","ex.b2.its-time-and-would-rather.08","ex.b2.its-time-and-would-rather.09","ex.b2.its-time-and-would-rather.10","ex.b2.its-time-and-would-rather.11","ex.b2.its-time-and-would-rather.12","ex.b2.its-time-and-would-rather.13","ex.b2.its-time-and-would-rather.14"]},{"id":"lesson.b2.mixed-conditionals","level":"B2","topicId":"topic.conditionals","title":"Mixed conditionals","goal":"Mixed conditionals combine past and present tenses in 'if' sentences to show how a past action affects the present, or how a present situation affects the past.","minutes":10,"grammarPointIds":["gp.mixed-conditionals"],"soundIds":[],"exerciseIds":["ex.b2.mixed-conditionals.01","ex.b2.mixed-conditionals.02","ex.b2.mixed-conditionals.03","ex.b2.mixed-conditionals.04","ex.b2.mixed-conditionals.05","ex.b2.mixed-conditionals.06","ex.b2.mixed-conditionals.07","ex.b2.mixed-conditionals.08","ex.b2.mixed-conditionals.09","ex.b2.mixed-conditionals.11","ex.b2.mixed-conditionals.12","ex.b2.mixed-conditionals.13","ex.b2.mixed-conditionals.14"]},{"id":"lesson.b2.modal-perfects-for-deduction","level":"B2","topicId":"topic.modals","title":"Must have, might have, can't have","goal":"Use 'must have', 'might have', and 'can't have' to make deductions or guesses about things that happened in the past.","minutes":11,"grammarPointIds":["gp.modal-perfects-for-deduction"],"soundIds":[],"exerciseIds":["ex.b2.modal-perfects-for-deduction.01","ex.b2.modal-perfects-for-deduction.03","ex.b2.modal-perfects-for-deduction.04","ex.b2.modal-perfects-for-deduction.08","ex.b2.modal-perfects-for-deduction.09","ex.b2.modal-perfects-for-deduction.10","ex.b2.modal-perfects-for-deduction.11","ex.b2.modal-perfects-for-deduction.12","ex.b2.modal-perfects-for-deduction.13","ex.b2.modal-perfects-for-deduction.14","ex.b2.modal-perfects-for-deduction.15","ex.b2.modal-perfects-for-deduction.16","ex.b2.modal-perfects-for-deduction.17","ex.b2.modal-perfects-for-deduction.18"]},{"id":"lesson.b2.non-defining-relative-clauses","level":"B2","topicId":"topic.relative-clauses","title":"Non-defining relative clauses","goal":"Non-defining relative clauses give extra, non-essential information about a noun, always separated by commas.","minutes":10,"grammarPointIds":["gp.non-defining-relative-clauses"],"soundIds":[],"exerciseIds":["ex.b2.non-defining-relative-clauses.01","ex.b2.non-defining-relative-clauses.02","ex.b2.non-defining-relative-clauses.03","ex.b2.non-defining-relative-clauses.04","ex.b2.non-defining-relative-clauses.05","ex.b2.non-defining-relative-clauses.06","ex.b2.non-defining-relative-clauses.07","ex.b2.non-defining-relative-clauses.10","ex.b2.non-defining-relative-clauses.11","ex.b2.non-defining-relative-clauses.12","ex.b2.non-defining-relative-clauses.13","ex.b2.non-defining-relative-clauses.14"]},{"id":"lesson.b2.order-of-adjectives","level":"B2","topicId":"topic.adjectives-adverbs","title":"Order of adjectives","goal":"Learn the common order for adjectives like opinion, size, age, color, origin, and material when describing a noun.","minutes":12,"grammarPointIds":["gp.order-of-adjectives"],"soundIds":[],"exerciseIds":["ex.b2.order-of-adjectives.01","ex.b2.order-of-adjectives.02","ex.b2.order-of-adjectives.03","ex.b2.order-of-adjectives.04","ex.b2.order-of-adjectives.06","ex.b2.order-of-adjectives.07","ex.b2.order-of-adjectives.10","ex.b2.order-of-adjectives.11","ex.b2.order-of-adjectives.12","ex.b2.order-of-adjectives.13","ex.b2.order-of-adjectives.14","ex.b2.order-of-adjectives.15","ex.b2.order-of-adjectives.16","ex.b2.order-of-adjectives.17","ex.b2.order-of-adjectives.18"]},{"id":"lesson.b2.participle-clauses","level":"B2","topicId":"topic.clauses","title":"Participle clauses","goal":"Participle clauses use -ing or -ed forms of verbs to add information about reason, time, or result, making sentences more concise.","minutes":10,"grammarPointIds":["gp.participle-clauses"],"soundIds":[],"exerciseIds":["ex.b2.participle-clauses.01","ex.b2.participle-clauses.02","ex.b2.participle-clauses.03","ex.b2.participle-clauses.04","ex.b2.participle-clauses.06","ex.b2.participle-clauses.08","ex.b2.participle-clauses.09","ex.b2.participle-clauses.10","ex.b2.participle-clauses.11","ex.b2.participle-clauses.12","ex.b2.participle-clauses.13","ex.b2.participle-clauses.14"]},{"id":"lesson.b2.passive-all-tenses","level":"B2","topicId":"topic.passive","title":"The passive in all tenses","goal":"Learn how to form and use the passive voice in different tenses, including continuous, perfect, and modal forms, to focus on the action or object.","minutes":10,"grammarPointIds":["gp.passive-all-tenses"],"soundIds":[],"exerciseIds":["ex.b2.passive-all-tenses.01","ex.b2.passive-all-tenses.02","ex.b2.passive-all-tenses.03","ex.b2.passive-all-tenses.04","ex.b2.passive-all-tenses.05","ex.b2.passive-all-tenses.06","ex.b2.passive-all-tenses.07","ex.b2.passive-all-tenses.08","ex.b2.passive-all-tenses.10","ex.b2.passive-all-tenses.11","ex.b2.passive-all-tenses.12","ex.b2.passive-all-tenses.13","ex.b2.passive-all-tenses.14"]},{"id":"lesson.b2.purpose-clauses","level":"B2","topicId":"topic.clauses","title":"So that and in order to","goal":"Learn to use 'so that' and 'in order to' to explain the purpose or reason behind an action.","minutes":12,"grammarPointIds":["gp.purpose-clauses"],"soundIds":[],"exerciseIds":["ex.b2.purpose-clauses.01","ex.b2.purpose-clauses.02","ex.b2.purpose-clauses.03","ex.b2.purpose-clauses.04","ex.b2.purpose-clauses.08","ex.b2.purpose-clauses.09","ex.b2.purpose-clauses.10","ex.b2.purpose-clauses.11","ex.b2.purpose-clauses.12","ex.b2.purpose-clauses.13","ex.b2.purpose-clauses.14","ex.b2.purpose-clauses.15","ex.b2.purpose-clauses.16","ex.b2.purpose-clauses.17","ex.b2.purpose-clauses.18"]},{"id":"lesson.b2.remember-stop-try-forget","level":"B2","topicId":"topic.verb-patterns","title":"Verbs that change meaning","goal":"Learn how verbs like 'remember,' 'stop,' 'try,' and 'forget' change meaning when followed by an -ing form or a to-infinitive.","minutes":13,"grammarPointIds":["gp.remember-stop-try-forget"],"soundIds":[],"exerciseIds":["ex.b2.remember-stop-try-forget.01","ex.b2.remember-stop-try-forget.02","ex.b2.remember-stop-try-forget.03","ex.b2.remember-stop-try-forget.04","ex.b2.remember-stop-try-forget.05","ex.b2.remember-stop-try-forget.06","ex.b2.remember-stop-try-forget.07","ex.b2.remember-stop-try-forget.08","ex.b2.remember-stop-try-forget.09","ex.b2.remember-stop-try-forget.11","ex.b2.remember-stop-try-forget.12","ex.b2.remember-stop-try-forget.14","ex.b2.remember-stop-try-forget.15","ex.b2.remember-stop-try-forget.16","ex.b2.remember-stop-try-forget.17","ex.b2.remember-stop-try-forget.18"]},{"id":"lesson.b2.reporting-passive","level":"B2","topicId":"topic.passive","title":"It is said that, he is thought to","goal":"Use passive structures like 'It is said that' or 'He is thought to' to report general beliefs, claims, or opinions without stating who said them.","minutes":11,"grammarPointIds":["gp.reporting-passive"],"soundIds":[],"exerciseIds":["ex.b2.reporting-passive.01","ex.b2.reporting-passive.02","ex.b2.reporting-passive.03","ex.b2.reporting-passive.04","ex.b2.reporting-passive.09","ex.b2.reporting-passive.10","ex.b2.reporting-passive.11","ex.b2.reporting-passive.12","ex.b2.reporting-passive.13","ex.b2.reporting-passive.14","ex.b2.reporting-passive.15","ex.b2.reporting-passive.16","ex.b2.reporting-passive.17","ex.b2.reporting-passive.18"]},{"id":"lesson.b2.reporting-verbs","level":"B2","topicId":"topic.reported-speech","title":"Reporting verbs","goal":"Learn how to use specific reporting verbs like 'suggest', 'deny', 'admit', 'warn', and 'refuse' with their correct grammatical patterns.","minutes":13,"grammarPointIds":["gp.reporting-verbs"],"soundIds":[],"exerciseIds":["ex.b2.reporting-verbs.01","ex.b2.reporting-verbs.02","ex.b2.reporting-verbs.03","ex.b2.reporting-verbs.04","ex.b2.reporting-verbs.07","ex.b2.reporting-verbs.08","ex.b2.reporting-verbs.09","ex.b2.reporting-verbs.10","ex.b2.reporting-verbs.11","ex.b2.reporting-verbs.12","ex.b2.reporting-verbs.13","ex.b2.reporting-verbs.14","ex.b2.reporting-verbs.15","ex.b2.reporting-verbs.16","ex.b2.reporting-verbs.17","ex.b2.reporting-verbs.18"]},{"id":"lesson.b2.should-have-and-could-have","level":"B2","topicId":"topic.modals","title":"Should have and could have","goal":"Use 'should have' to express regret or criticism about past actions, and 'could have' for missed opportunities or possibilities that didn't happen.","minutes":11,"grammarPointIds":["gp.should-have-and-could-have"],"soundIds":[],"exerciseIds":["ex.b2.should-have-and-could-have.01","ex.b2.should-have-and-could-have.02","ex.b2.should-have-and-could-have.03","ex.b2.should-have-and-could-have.04","ex.b2.should-have-and-could-have.05","ex.b2.should-have-and-could-have.06","ex.b2.should-have-and-could-have.07","ex.b2.should-have-and-could-have.08","ex.b2.should-have-and-could-have.09","ex.b2.should-have-and-could-have.10","ex.b2.should-have-and-could-have.11","ex.b2.should-have-and-could-have.12","ex.b2.should-have-and-could-have.13","ex.b2.should-have-and-could-have.14"]},{"id":"lesson.b2.the-more-the-better","level":"B2","topicId":"topic.comparison","title":"The more ..., the better","goal":"Use \"The more..., the more...\" or \"The more..., the less...\" to show how two things change together proportionally.","minutes":